                                                                  2631 1451   25
 ENDF/B-VI MOD 3 Evaluation, September 1996, M.B. Chadwick,       2631 1451   26
                 P.G. Young (LANL) and A.J. Koning (ECN)          2631 1451   27
                                                                  2631 1451   28
 Los Alamos LA150 Library, produced with FKK/GNASH/GSCAN code     2631 1451   29
   in cooperation with ECN Petten.                                2631 1451   30
                                                                  2631 1451   31
  This evaluation provides a complete representation of the       2631 1451   32
nuclear data needed for transport, damage, heating,               2631 1451   33
radioactivity, and shielding applications over the incident       2631 1451   34
neutron energy range from 1.0E-11 to 150 MeV.  The discussion     2631 1451   35
here is divided into the region below and above 20 MeV.           2631 1451   36
                                                                  2631 1451   37
INCIDENT NEUTRON ENERGIES < 20 MeV                                2631 1451   38
                                                                  2631 1451   39
  Below 20 MeV the evaluation is based completely on the ENDF/B-  2631 1451   40
VI.1 (Release 1) evaluation by Fu91 (see also Fu86).  The         2631 1451   41
following modifications were made to the ENDF/B-VI.1 evaluation:  2631 1451   42
                                                                  2631 1451   43
1.  The covariance files (MF=33) were removed from the file.      2631 1451   44
                                                                  2631 1451   45
2.  The derived MF=3 files for MT=203,205,207 were removed.       2631 1451   46
                                                                  2631 1451   47
INCIDENT NEUTRON ENERGIES > 20 MeV                                2631 1451   48
                                                                  2631 1451   49
  The evaluation above 20 MeV utilizes MF=6, MT=5 to represent    2631 1451   50
all reaction data.  Production cross sections and emission        2631 1451   51
spectra are given for neutrons, protons, deuterons, tritons,      2631 1451   52
alpha particles, gamma rays, and all residual nuclides produced   2631 1451   53
(A>5) in the reaction chains. To summarize, the ENDF sections     2631 1451   54
with non-zero data above En = 20 MeV are:                         2631 1451   55
                                                                  2631 1451   56
  MF=3 MT=  1  Total Cross Section                                2631 1451   57
       MT=  2  Elastic Scattering Cross Section                   2631 1451   58
       MT=  3  Nonelastic Cross Section                           2631 1451   59
       MT=  5  Sum of Binary (n,n') and (n,x) Reactions           2631 1451   60
                                                                  2631 1451   61
  MF=4 MT=  2  Elastic Angular Distributions                      2631 1451   62
                                                                  2631 1451   63
  MF=6 MT=  5  Production Cross Sections and Energy-Angle         2631 1451   64
               Distributions for Emission Neutrons, Protons,      2631 1451   65
               Deuterons, and Alphas; and Angle-Integrated        2631 1451   66
               Spectra for Gamma Rays and Residual Nuclei That    2631 1451   67
               Are Stable Against Particle Emission               2631 1451   68
                                                                  2631 1451   69
  The evaluation is based on nuclear model calculations that      2631 1451   70
have been benchmarked to experimental data, especially for n +    2631 1451   71
Fe56 and p + Fe56 reactions [Ch96a]. We use the GNASH code        2631 1451   72
system [Yo92], which utilizes Hauser-Feshbach statistical,        2631 1451   73
preequilibrium and direct-reaction theories. Spherical optical    2631 1451   74
model calculations are used to obtain particle transmission       2631 1451   75
coefficients for the Hauser-Feshbach calculations, as well as     2631 1451   76
for the elastic neutron angular distributions.                    2631 1451   77
  Cross sections and spectra for producing individual residual    2631 1451   78
nuclei are included for reactions that exceed a cross section of  2631 1451   79
approximately 1 nb at any energy. The energy-angle-correlations   2631 1451   80
for all outgoing particles are based on Kalbach systematics       2631 1451   81
[Ka88].                                                           2631 1451   82
  A model was developed to calculate the energy distributions of  2631 1451   83
all recoil nuclei in the GNASH calculations [Ch96b]. The recoil   2631 1451   84
energy distributions are represented in the laboratory system in  2631 1451   85
MT=5, MF=6, and are given as isotropic in the lab system. Note    2631 1451   86
that all other data in MT=5,MF=6 are given in the center-of-mass  2631 1451   87
system. This method of representation requires a modification of  2631 1451   88
the original ENDF-6 format.                                       2631 1451   89
  Preequilibrium corrections were performed in the course of the  2631 1451   90
GNASH calculations using the exciton model of Kalbach [Ka77,      2631 1451   91
Ka85], validated by comparison with calculations using Feshbach,  2631 1451   92
Kerman, Koonin (FKK) theory [Ch93].  Discrete level data from     2631 1451   93
nuclear data sheets were matched to continuum level densities     2631 1451   94
using the formulation of Ignatyuk [Ig75] and pairing and shell    2631 1451   95
parameters from the Cook [Co67] analysis. Neutron and charged-    2631 1451   96
particle transmission coefficients were obtained from the         2631 1451   97
optical potentials, as discussed below.  Gamma-ray transmission   2631 1451   98
coefficients were calculated using the Kopecky-Uhl model [Ko90].  2631 1451   99
  The neutron optical model potential of Arthur et al. [Ar80]     2631 1451  100
was used to calculate transmission coefficients and cross         2631 1451  101
sections with the SCAT2 code [Be92] up to a neutron energy of 26  2631 1451  102
MeV.  Between 26 and 52 MeV, the imaginary volume component of    2631 1451  103
Arthur's potential was modified to better account for nonelastic  2631 1451  104
cross section measurements, and above 52 MeV the Semmering        2631 1451  105
potential of Madland [Ma88] was used.  For protons, the           2631 1451  106
Beccetti-Greenlees potential [Be69] was utilized below 28 MeV,    2631 1451  107
and the Madland potential [Ma88] was used at higher energies.     2631 1451  108
The global spherical potential of Perey [Pe63] was utilized for   2631 1451  109
deuterons, and the potential of Beccetti-Greenlees [Be71]         2631 1451  110
was adopted for tritons.  Finally, the alpha potential of Lemos   2631 1451  111
[Le72], as adapted by Arthur et al. [Ar80], was used for alpha    2631 1451  112
particles.                                                        2631 1451  113
  Direct reaction cross sections to discrete states were          2631 1451  114
calculated with the ECIS95 code [Re95] using deformation          2631 1451  115
parameters compiled in Nuclear Data Sheets.                       2631 1451  116
